Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?inline-3d(#143):=20the=20Android=20per-piece=20?=
 =?UTF-8?q?repack=20=E2=80=94=20lift=200114's=20gate?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Patch 0114 gave overlapping inline-3D tiles a guillotine decomposition and a
per-piece REPACK on Windows/macOS, and gated Android OFF: its weave input is
not filled by a provider blit at all, so it had nowhere to hang a repack, and
handing that arm N destination rects without one would have sliced the
side-by-side midline (comp_multi_weave_android.c splits each submitted rect at
its own INTEGER midpoint, half = rw / 2) and corrupted its eyes — exactly the
defect browser#131 and #143 exist to kill. Composition case 12 therefore still
looked broken on the device: the whole earthview row band at the helmet's
height flat, including the parts nothing occludes.

WHERE THE REPACK LIVES, AND WHY NOT IN THE PROVIDER. Android has no per-target
scratch (0112 excludes it for that reason) and no second copy: viz Skia-DRAWS
each element straight into the shared window-sized staging image at its own
window rect. So the repack goes into that draw. For each WeaveCopy the caller
already computed for the desktop arms, the staging draw is re-run CLIPPED to
the copy's destination half and TRANSLATED so that half's source columns —
taken from the virtual full-tile pair this draw already knows how to paint,
never from the shared staging input, which is precisely the collision 0114
dissolved — land in it. |rc.dst| and |rect.origin() + rc.src| are the same
SIZE, so the map is a pure integer translation: no rescale, no resample, and
therefore no new filtering question for either the backdrop (linear 2:1
downscale) or the SBS (nearest 1:1). It covers the browser#30 backdrop
pre-blend for free, which a hand-written per-piece formula would have had to
get right twice; the backdrop snapshot is hoisted so N passes cost one
makeImageSnapshot, not N.

Clip first, translate second — the clip is stated in the untranslated space,
which is the space |rc.dst| lives in.

THE NO-OVERLAP PATH IS BYTE-IDENTICAL. |single_full_width| — one piece
spanning the tile's full width, which is every frame with no overlap at all
AND every tile demoted to 0113's row band — takes ZERO repack passes: one
unclipped, untranslated draw and one WeaveRectAndroid, i.e. bit-for-bit the
pre-patch code. That is 0113's standard, 0114's standard, and this patch's.

The submit half is DisplayXRWeaveGpuAndroid::WeaveRectsAndroid: one batch entry
per piece, srcRect == dstRect, no wire-protocol change. It validates every rect
before pushing the first (a half-staged decomposition reads on the panel as a
hole, not as a failure) and ERASES |batch_last_copied_| rather than updating it
— after a repack the staging content at an address is a function of the whole
decomposition, so replaying one recorded rect is unsound. Same reasoning, same
shape as 0114's BatchCopyPiecesFrom.

Everything else is 0114's, unchanged and deliberately not re-derived: the
even-cut rule (DisplayXRSnapOccluderX — mandatory here, because Android is one
of the two arms whose integer rw / 2 makes the right eye a texel wider on an
odd width), the 8-rects-per-tile cap, the 32-rects-per-frame budget with
largest-first demotion (ONE SubmitBatchSync per frame: every submit runs the
runtime's process_atlas once, and N of those starve the SR predictor), the odd
residual column going to the mono leg, and DisplayXRBuildRepack refusing a
piece it cannot express (a piece narrower than 2px has hw == 0 and is refused,
then demoted — an assertion with a safe landing, unreachable after the snap).

SELF-VERIFICATION, because a demotion is never anonymous:
  [DXR-REPACK] android per-piece repack (browser#143) ENABLED   (once, at startup)
  [DXR-REPACK] tiles=N pieces=N demoted_cap=N demoted_budget=N refused_odd=N
one line per frame while any decomposition is active, with the three demotion
causes counted SEPARATELY because they want different fixes. Android-scoped:
the Windows arm shipped with 0114 and its log volume is not this patch's to
change.

KILL SWITCH: --disable-inline-3d-android-repack, read once in viz (default ON)
and forwarded to the GPU process by ChromeContentBrowserClient. Negative rather
than positive so the escape hatch works even where nobody appended a positive
switch; the name follows this fork's --disable-inline-3d-occlusion convention.

0114's in-code gate comment is UPDATED, not deleted: it now says the repack
exists and points at it.

NOT DONE: no device testing (not this change's job), nothing pushed, no PR.
